{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|tin}}, from {{inh|en|ang|tin}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*tin}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*tiną}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|tĭn}}, {{IPA|en|/tɪn/|[tʰɪn]}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-tin.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{audio|en|En-tin.ogg|Audio (UK)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪn|s=1}}
* {{homophones|en|thin|q1=with [[th-stopping]]|ten|q2=with [[pin-pen merger]]}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} A [[malleable]], [[ductile]], [[metal]]lic [[element]], resistant to [[corrosion]], with atomic number 50 and symbol Sn.
# {{lb|en|NZ|AU|South African|Irish|British|countable}} An airtight container, made of tin or another metal, used to preserve food, or hold a liquid or some other product.
#: {{ux|en|a '''tin''' of baked beans; a tobacco '''tin'''; a '''tin''' of shoe polish}}
#: {{ux|en|Several '''tins''' of paint were needed to paint the house.}}
#: {{ux|en|empty '''tins''', cans, and plastic containers are recycled in the blue bins.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1943|month=November and December|author=G. T. Porter|title=The Lines Behind the Lines in Burma|journal=Railway Magazine|page=325|text=When it arrived, the train was headed by a "K" class 4-6-0 wood-burning locomotive, and a water-tank wagon next to the tender was immediately besieged by women and girls, clad in their picturesque national costume, all with empty kerosene '''tins''' for water, a scene which was re-enacted at each stop down the line.}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} A metal [[pan]] used for baking, roasting, etc.
#: {{ux|en|muffin '''tin'''}}
#: {{ux|en|roasting '''tin'''}}
#: {{ux|en|baking '''tin'''}}
# {{lb|en|countable|squash}} The bottom part of the [[front wall]], which is "out" if a player strikes it with the [[squash ball|ball]].
# {{lb|en|slang|dated|uncountable}} [[money]], especially [[silver]] money.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1844|author=w:Benjamin Disraeli|title={{w|Coningsby (novel)|Coningsby}}
|passage=The father is a cotton lord, and they all have loads of '''tin''', you know}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1861|author=Philip William Perfitt|title=The Pathfinder|page=377
|passage=When all your '''tin''' is gone and spent, / And you've not a [[mag]] for bread or rent}}
# {{lb|en|slang|uncountable}} [[computer]] [[hardware]].

